-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 1: The Atheist's § 68 Paradox
--
-- § 68: "Ingen er pligtig at yde personlige bidrag til nogen ANDEN
--  gudsdyrkelse end den, som er HANS EGEN."
--
-- You don't have to pay for OTHERS' worship. Only your OWN.
-- The word "anden" (other) presupposes you HAVE a worship.
-- What if you don't?
--
-- An atheist has NO "own" worship. ALL worship is "other" to them.
-- § 68 says you don't pay for other worship than your own.
-- For an atheist, ALL worship is other.
-- Therefore: an atheist cannot be constitutionally obligated
-- to fund ANY religious activity.
--
-- But § 4 says the state must support the Folkekirke.
-- State support comes from taxes. Atheists pay taxes.
-- An atheist's taxes partially fund the Folkekirke.
--
-- § 68 says they shouldn't have to.
-- § 4 says the state must.
-- The atheist is a constitutional edge case that the 1953 drafters
-- didn't consider — because they assumed everyone worships God (§ 67).
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 1: The Atheist's § 68 Paradox ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 68: 'nogen ANDEN gudsdyrkelse end den, som er hans egen'")
@ print("  Presupposes you HAVE an 'own' worship.")
@ print("")
@ print("  An atheist:")
@ print("    Has own worship? NEJ")
@ print("    Then ALL worship is 'anden' (other)")
@ print("    § 68 says: you don't pay for 'anden' worship")
@ print("    Therefore: an atheist cannot be obligated to fund ANY religion")
@ print("")
@ print("  But:")
@ print("    § 4: state MUST support Folkekirken")
@ print("    State support = taxes")
@ print("    Atheists pay taxes")
@ print("    Atheist taxes fund the Folkekirke")
@ print("    § 68 says they shouldn't have to")
@ print("")
@ print("  The constitution presumes theism (§ 67 'dyrke Gud').")
@ print("  An atheist breaks the assumption. Edge case: the drafters")
@ print("  didn't imagine someone with NO 'own' worship.")
@ print("")


-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 2: The Two-Tier Liberty System
--
-- The constitution carefully modulates WHO has which rights:
--
--   UNIVERSAL ("enhver", "ingen"):
--     § 70: Equality (creed/descent) — "Ingen"
--     § 77: Free expression — "Enhver"
--
--   CITIZEN-SPECIFIC ("dansk borger", "borgerne"):
--     § 71: Personal liberty — "Ingen DANSK BORGER"
--     § 78: Association — "Borgerne"
--     § 79: Assembly — "Borgerne"
--
-- The result: a foreigner in Denmark has:
--   ✓ Equal treatment (§ 70)
--   ✓ Free speech (§ 77)
--   ✗ Full personal liberty protection (§ 71)
--   ✗ Association freedom (§ 78)
--   ✗ Assembly freedom (§ 79)
--
-- You can SAY anything but can't MEET freely.
-- You can PUBLISH but can't ORGANIZE.
--
-- This is not accidental — the drafters chose different words
-- for different rights. The constitution has a SUBJECT GRAMMAR.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 2: The Two-Tier Liberty System ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  UNIVERSAL (all persons):")
@ print("    § 70: 'Ingen' — equality")
@ print("    § 77: 'Enhver' — free expression")
@ print("")
@ print("  CITIZEN-SPECIFIC:")
@ print("    § 71: 'Ingen DANSK BORGER' — personal liberty")
@ print("    § 78: 'Borgerne' — association")
@ print("    § 79: 'Borgerne' — assembly")
@ print("")
@ print("  A foreigner in Denmark:")
@ print("    Can speak freely (§ 77): JA")
@ print("    Equal treatment (§ 70): JA")
@ print("    Full habeas corpus (§ 71): NEJ")
@ print("    Form associations (§ 78): NEJ")
@ print("    Assemble freely (§ 79): NEJ")
@ print("")
@ print("  You can PUBLISH but can't ORGANIZE.")
@ print("  You can SAY anything but can't MEET freely.")
@ print("  The constitution has a SUBJECT GRAMMAR.")
@ print("")


-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 3: The Morality Monopoly
--
-- § 67 limits religion by "sædeligheden" (morality).
-- § 63 gives courts the power to review everything.
-- § 4 makes the Folkekirke the state church.
--
-- Who DEFINES morality in practice?
-- Courts do (§ 63). But courts exist in a culture shaped by...
-- the Folkekirke (state church for 500+ years).
--
-- The state religion limits other religions by morality.
-- The morality standard is culturally set by the state religion.
-- The regulator is the competitor.
--
-- It's as if McDonald's wrote the health code for Burger King.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 3: The Morality Monopoly ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 67: Religion limited by 'saedeligheden' (morality)")
@ print("  § 63: Courts define the limits of authority")
@ print("  § 4:  Folkekirken = state church for 500+ years")
@ print("")
@ print("  Who defines 'saedelighed'?")
@ print("    Formally: domstolene (§ 63)")
@ print("    Culturally: 500 years of Folkekirke influence")
@ print("")
@ print("  The state religion's culture shapes the morality standard.")
@ print("  The morality standard limits other religions.")
@ print("  The regulator is the competitor.")
@ print("  McDonald's writes the health code for Burger King.")
@ print("")


-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 4: § 80 — The King Protects You From Himself
--
-- § 80: Armed force against crowds can only proceed
-- "i kongens og lovens navn" — in the King's and the law's name.
--
-- But:
--   § 12: The King is supreme commander of the military
--   § 80: Military force must be announced in the King's name
--
-- The military invokes the King's authority to LIMIT the King's
-- military. The King's NAME is the restraint on the King's POWER.
--
-- You are being protected from the sovereign BY the sovereign's name.
-- The formula requires the crowd to hear that the King himself
-- authorizes the limitation of his own force.
--
-- It's as if a bouncer says "the boss says you can stay."
-- The boss IS the one who sent the bouncer.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 4: The King Protects You From Himself ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 12: Kongen is supreme military commander")
@ print("  § 80: Force proceeds 'i kongens og lovens navn'")
@ print("")
@ print("  The military invokes the King's authority")
@ print("  to LIMIT the King's military.")
@ print("  The King's NAME is the restraint on the King's POWER.")
@ print("")
@ print("  The bouncer says 'the boss says you can stay.'")
@ print("  The boss IS the one who sent the bouncer.")
@ print("")


-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 5: § 81 — The Conscientious Objection Gap
--
-- "Enhver våbenfør mand er forpligtet til MED SIN PERSON
--  at bidrage til fædrelandets forsvar."
--
-- "Med sin person" — with his PERSON. His body.
-- Not "with his labor." Not "with his resources." His BODY.
--
-- The constitution mandates PHYSICAL military service.
-- There is NO exception for conscience. No alternative service.
-- The right to refuse military service on moral grounds is
-- NOT in the constitution — it's a creation of ordinary law.
--
-- If Folketing repealed the conscientious objection law tomorrow,
-- § 81 would stand alone: every able-bodied man MUST serve.
-- Period. No alternative. The constitution provides no basis
-- for refusal. It's a pure physical obligation.
--
-- Cross-ref § 70: no one shall evade duties based on creed.
-- § 81 demands physical service. § 70 says you can't refuse
-- a duty because of your religion. Together: you cannot
-- religiously object to military service (constitutionally).
--
-- Denmark allows it anyway — but by ORDINARY LAW,
-- which is weaker than the constitution that commands service.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 5: The Conscientious Objection Gap ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 81: 'MED SIN PERSON at bidrage til forsvaret'")
@ print("  = with his BODY. Not labor. Not money. His body.")
@ print("")
@ print("  The constitution says: you MUST serve. Physically.")
@ print("  No exception for conscience. No alternative service.")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 70: 'unddrage sig opfyldelsen af nogen borgerpligt'")
@ print("  = you CANNOT evade a duty because of your creed")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 81 + § 70 together:")
@ print("    You must serve (§ 81)")
@ print("    You cannot refuse due to religion (§ 70)")
@ print("    = NO constitutional basis for conscientious objection")
@ print("")
@ print("  Denmark allows it — by ORDINARY LAW.")
@ print("  If that law were repealed, § 81 stands alone.")
@ print("  The constitution COMMANDS service and FORBIDS religious refusal.")
@ print("")


-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 6: Denmark Has Nobles With No Nobility
--
-- § 83: "til adel, titel og rang knyttet FORRET er afskaffet"
-- Privileges ARE abolished. But adel (nobility) is not.
-- Titles persist. Privileges don't.
--
-- There are still Danish counts and barons.
-- They have titles. They have no constitutional privileges.
-- A "greve" (count) has exactly the same rights as any citizen.
-- The title is an ornamental word with zero legal content.
--
-- This is the most Danish possible solution:
-- Instead of dramatically ABOLISHING the aristocracy,
-- the constitution simply EMPTIED it.
-- The nobles still exist. They're just normal people with fancy names.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 6: Nobles With No Nobility ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 83 abolishes FORRET (privilege), not ADEL (nobility)")
@ print("  Danish counts and barons still exist")
@ print("  They have titles. They have zero privileges.")
@ print("  A 'greve' has exactly the same rights as everyone else.")
@ print("")
@ print("  The most Danish solution:")
@ print("  Don't abolish the aristocracy — just EMPTY it.")
@ print("  The nobles still exist. They're normal people with fancy names.")
@ print("")


-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 7: § 77's Enumeration Paradox — Is Code Speech?
--
-- § 77: "på tryk, i skrift og tale" — print, writing, speech.
-- Three modes. An EXHAUSTIVE or ILLUSTRATIVE list?
--
-- If exhaustive:
--   Code is not print, writing, or speech → not protected
--   A painting is not print, writing, or speech → not protected
--   A YouTube video MIGHT be "tale" → maybe protected
--   Mime is not speech → not protected
--   Sign language is not "tale" (verbal speech) → not protected?
--
-- If illustrative: why enumerate at all?
--
-- The 1849 drafters named the media they knew.
-- In 2026, the most important speech acts happen in media
-- they never imagined.
--
-- BONUS: "Censur og andre forebyggende forholdsregler kan
-- INGENSINDE PÅNY indføres."
-- If § 77 only covers print/writing/speech, then CENSORSHIP
-- of images, film, and code was never banned by "ingensinde."
-- You could constitutionally censor a film but not a book.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 7: Is Code Speech? ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 77: 'paa tryk, i skrift og tale' — 3 modes enumerated")
@ print("")
@ print("  If the list is EXHAUSTIVE:")
@ print("    Code: not protected")
@ print("    Paintings: not protected")
@ print("    Sign language: not protected?")
@ print("    Film: not protected")
@ print("    YouTube: maybe ('tale'?)")
@ print("    Mime: definitely not protected")
@ print("")
@ print("  The censorship ban ('ingensinde paany') applies to § 77.")
@ print("  If § 77 only covers print/writing/speech, then:")
@ print("    Censoring a BOOK = unconstitutional")
@ print("    Censoring a FILM = constitutionally allowed?")
@ print("    Censoring CODE = constitutionally allowed?")
@ print("")
@ print("  Modern interpretation says no. But the TEXT says what it says.")
@ print("")


-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 8: The § 79 Pre-Crime Ban
--
-- § 79: Outdoor assemblies can be banned when danger is "befrygtes"
-- (feared/anticipated).
--
-- This is a PRE-CRIME standard. Not "when danger exists" but
-- "when danger can be feared." The constitutional threshold for
-- banning an assembly is based on PREDICTION, not fact.
--
-- Who does the fearing? The police.
-- What counts as "befrygtning"? Undefined.
--
-- This means: the RIGHT to assemble outdoors exists, but the
-- EXCEPTION is based on subjective anticipation.
-- A peaceful protest can be constitutionally banned if the police
-- FEAR it might turn violent.
--
-- Compare § 71 stk. 5: no pretrial detention for fine-only offenses.
-- The constitution won't lock you up before trial for small crimes,
-- but WILL ban your gathering before it happens.
-- Preventive detention: NO. Preventive assembly ban: YES.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 8: The § 79 Pre-Crime Ban ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 79: 'naar der af dem kan BEFRYGTES fare'")
@ print("  = when danger can be FEARED. Not exists — feared.")
@ print("")
@ print("  This is a PRE-CRIME standard for outdoor assemblies.")
@ print("  A peaceful protest can be banned if police FEAR violence.")
@ print("")
@ print("  Compare § 71 stk. 5:")
@ print("    Preventive detention for minor crimes: NEJ")
@ print("    Preventive assembly ban: JA")
@ print("  The constitution won't lock you up based on prediction")
@ print("  but WILL ban your gathering based on prediction.")
@ print("")


-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 9: The § 75 Disability Trap
--
-- § 75 stk. 1: "enhver ARBEJDSDUELIG borger" — every ABLE-TO-WORK citizen
-- § 75 stk. 2: help for those who "ikke selv kan ernære sig eller sine"
--   BUT ONLY IF "forsørgelse ikke påhviler nogen anden"
--
-- A disabled person:
--   stk. 1 doesn't apply (not "arbejdsduelig" / able to work)
--   stk. 2 applies — but only if NO ONE ELSE is responsible
--
-- If a disabled person has a wealthy family:
--   stk. 1: irrelevant (can't work)
--   stk. 2: no public help (family is "anden" / another who's responsible)
--
-- The constitution says: if your family is wealthy, you have
-- NO constitutional right to public assistance, even if disabled.
-- The safety net has a FAMILY-RESPONSIBILITY GATE.
--
-- The welfare state we know is built on ORDINARY LAW.
-- The constitution only guarantees help as LAST RESORT.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 9: The § 75 Disability Trap ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 75.1: 'enhver ARBEJDSDUELIG borger' — only able-bodied")
@ print("  § 75.2: help IF you can't support 'sig eller sine'")
@ print("          AND IF 'forsoergelse ikke paahviler nogen ANDEN'")
@ print("")
@ print("  Disabled person with wealthy family:")
@ print("    § 75.1: doesn't apply (not arbejdsduelig)")
@ print("    § 75.2: no help (family is 'nogen anden')")
@ print("    Result: NO constitutional right to public assistance")
@ print("")
@ print("  The Danish welfare state is built on ORDINARY LAW.")
@ print("  The constitution only guarantees help as LAST RESORT.")
@ print("  A simple majority could constitutionally dismantle welfare")
@ print("  for anyone who has a family member with resources.")
@ print("")


-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 10: The Complete Subject Grammar
--
-- Collecting ALL the subject words across §§ 66-85:
--
-- "Borgerne" (the citizens): §§ 67, 78, 79
-- "Ingen" (nobody): §§ 68, 70
-- "Ingen dansk borger" (no Danish citizen): § 71
-- "Enhver" (everyone): §§ 71.3, 77, 81, 83
-- "Den, der..." (the one who...): § 75.2
-- "Alle børn" (all children): § 76
-- "Kommunerne" (municipalities): § 82
-- "Enhver våbenfør mand" (every able-bodied man): § 81
--
-- The constitution distinguishes at least 6 CATEGORIES of subject:
--   1. All persons (enhver)
--   2. All citizens (borgerne)
--   3. Danish citizens specifically (dansk borger)
--   4. Specific populations (børn, mænd, kommuner)
--   5. Nobody / no-one (ingen) — universal prohibition
--   6. Conditional (den, der...)
--
-- Each right is PRECISELY SCOPED to its intended beneficiary.
-- This is not sloppy drafting — it's a GRAMMAR OF RIGHTS.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 10: The Complete Subject Grammar ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  'Borgerne' (citizens): §§ 67, 78, 79")
@ print("  'Ingen' (nobody): §§ 68, 70")
@ print("  'Ingen dansk borger': § 71")
@ print("  'Enhver' (everyone): §§ 77, 81, 83")
@ print("  'Alle boern': § 76")
@ print("  'Enhver vaabenfoer mand': § 81")
@ print("  'Den, der...': § 75.2")
@ print("")
@ print("  6 categories of constitutional subject.")
@ print("  Each right precisely scoped to its beneficiary.")
@ print("  This is a GRAMMAR OF RIGHTS.")
@ print("")


-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 11: The "Hæfte" Ghost
--
-- § 71 stk. 5: "straf af bøde eller HÆFTE"
-- Hæfte was a form of short-term detention, ABOLISHED in 2001.
-- The constitutional text still references it.
--
-- This is a DEAD WORD in a living constitution.
-- The provision still applies (no pretrial detention for minor offenses)
-- but one of its two referenced penalties no longer exists.
--
-- Combined with § 72's "telegraf" and § 77's "tryk":
-- The constitution is full of 1953 technology and legal concepts.
-- Three ghosts: hæfte (abolished), telegraf (obsolete), tryk (narrowing).
-- The text ages. The principles don't. But the text IS the law.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 11: The 'Haefte' Ghost ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 71.5: 'straf af boede eller HAEFTE'")
@ print("  Haefte abolished in 2001. Still in the constitution.")
@ print("")
@ print("  Three ghosts in the constitutional text:")
@ print("    § 71.5: 'haefte' — abolished 2001")
@ print("    § 72:   'telegraf' — technology obsolete")
@ print("    § 77:   'tryk' — narrowing medium")
@ print("")
@ print("  The text ages. The principles don't.")
@ print("  But the text IS the law.")
@ print("")


